---
// Goal: introduce the talk and Beatles Bench as a place where every answer
// is known, and point to the pages that follow the talk.
import BeatlesPage from '../../../layouts/BeatlesPage.astro';
import SlideImage from '../../../components/SlideImage.astro';
import { FIRST, PAGES } from '../../../data/beatles.mjs';

const TALK = 'Jev, ThinkThen, and Beatles Bench';
const field = PAGES.find((p) => p.slug === 'bench-field');
---
<BeatlesPage page={{ ...FIRST, description: `The talk "${TALK}" as short pages: one slide and one idea each.` }}>
  <div class="eyebrow">Learn</div>
  <h1>Beatles Bench</h1>
  <p class="lede">These pages follow the talk "{TALK}", one slide at a time.</p>
  <figure class="slide">
    <a href="/learn/beatles-bench/beatles-bench.webp"><SlideImage src="/learn/beatles-bench/beatles-bench.webp" alt={`The talk's title slide: ${TALK}`} /></a>
  </figure>

  <p>Beatles Bench is the playground for the talk. It asks Jev questions about Beatles songs. A script set the right answer to each scored question ahead of time, from Wikipedia and Wikidata. The bench shows ThinkThen at work and shows where it goes wrong.</p>
  <p>Each page shows one slide and one idea. Most pages show a command and the same command at another bar. Every command answers from a saved recording. You need no key, and nothing leaves your machine.</p>
  <p>The slides show the talk's own runs. The examples replay on the current thinkthen. A page says so under its slide when the slide shows other output.</p>
  <p><a href={field.route}>{field.title.replace(/\.$/, '')}</a> gives the scores and shows how to get the bench and replay every answer.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/learn/beatles-bench/jev-thinkthen-beatles-bench.pdf">talk's slides as one PDF</a> hold every slide in order.</p>
  <p>It is not an official benchmark. Please don't tune a model on it. The bench's author also builds ThinkThen.</p>
</BeatlesPage>
